  "strategy": "The focus is to generate a step function with non-increasing maximum self-convolution. The previous program achieved an upper bound using iterative coordinate descent, Adam optimization, and LP mirror descent methods. To improve upon this, a multi-start gradient descent approach is proposed.\n\nThe new strategy involves:\n1. Representing the sequence as a vector where the length is fixed (1000 if no parent, otherwise parent length)\n2. Using gradient descent for the core optimization, evaluating with `evaluate_sequence`\n3. Employing a coordinate-wise update approach optimized by gradients\n4. Includes normalization and clipping steps for validity\n5. All states are converted to integers to enable efficient computation\n\nKey steps include:\n- Initialization from parent if available\n- Structured update process to find optimal updates\n- Step-by-step optimization\n\nMathematical formulation:\n- Input sequence represented as vector a\n- Objective designed to minimize max self-convolution over sum\n- Constraints: all elements >=0, <=1000, sum >=0.01\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Handle initialization\n2. Set up vectors for tracking\n3. Compute initial performance\n4. Iteratively update coordinates with local optimization\n\nThe approach carefully handles performance metrics and termination conditions while searching for improved sequences.\n\nSearch schedules:\n- Timed iterations ensuring efficiency\n- Prioritizes self-improvement steps\n\nBest results are tracked across candidate evolution.\n\nGradient handling:\n- Computations are derived from automatic differentiation\n- Updates are step-sized by the gradient's quality\n\nResampling:\n- Uses linear interpolation for length adjustments when beneficial\n\nValidation:\n- Consistent J-evaluation to prevent errors\n- Strict constraints maintenance\n\nTermination:\n- When improvement <= threshold, returns best found",
